# Reflection: Cloud Services, Git, and GitHub

Cloud computing has become a normal part of my everyday life.
  I use different cloud services without always thinking about how they work in the background.
  For example, I use **Google Drive** to store and access my files, **Gmail** for communication, 
  and **GitHub** to store and manage my projects. These services are useful because I can access
my files and applications from different devices as long as I have an Internet connection.

In this course, I learned about the three main cloud service models: **Infrastructure as a Service
(IaaS), Platform as a Service (PaaS), and Software as a Service (SaaS)**. IaaS provides resources
such as virtual machines, storage, and networking. PaaS provides a platform where developers can
create and deploy applications without having to manage everything underneath. SaaS provides
applications that users can access through the Internet. Understanding these models helped me
see that cloud computing is not just about online storage but also includes the infrastructure 
and platforms used to run applications.

One thing I found especially important is **Git**. Before learning about version control,
I mostly thought of saving different copies of files as a way to keep track of my work. Git 
provides a better way to manage changes because it keeps a history of the project. It allows 
me to see what was changed and makes it easier to go back to an earlier version when necessary.

I also learned that **GitHub** is useful for storing Git repositories online and working with other 
people. It can help organize projects, share files and code, and keep track of changes. For a course 
like Cloud Computing, I think GitHub is especially useful because we will be working with configurations, 
documentation, scripts, and different cloud technologies throughout the semester.

Overall, this activity helped me understand that cloud computing is more than just using online services. 
It involves different technologies and practices such as virtualization, servers, storage, containers, security,
monitoring, and automation. I also realized that Git and GitHub are important tools for keeping cloud-related projects
organized and maintaining a record of the work that has been done. As I continue learning in this course, I expect my GitHub
repository to become a useful record of the skills and projects I develop.
